<H1 class=3DTitle><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>3<BR><A=20
name=3DPFGRF003>Gathering Optimizer =
Statistics</A></FONT></H1><!--/TOC=3DTitle--><A=20
name=3D13548></A>
<P class=3DBP>This chapter explains why statistics are important for the =

cost-based optimizer and how to gather and use statistics.</P><A =
name=3D23330></A>
<P class=3DBP>The chapter contains the following sections:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D14956></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#25018">Understanding=20
  Statistics</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25289></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#26713">Generating=20
  Statistics</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25805></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#31058">Using=20
  Statistics</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D27237></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#27027">Using=20
  Histograms</A> </LI></UL><A name=3D25018></A><!--TOC=3Dh1-"25018"-->
<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Understanding Statistics</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A=20
name=3D26163></A>
<P class=3DBP>As database administrator, you can generate statistics =
that quantify=20
the data distribution and storage characteristics of tables, columns, =
indexes,=20
and partitions. The cost-based optimization approach uses these =
statistics to=20
calculate the selectivity of predicates and to estimate the cost of each =

execution plan. <STRONG class=3DGlossaryTerm>Selectivity</STRONG> is the =
fraction=20
of rows in a table that the SQL statement's predicate chooses. The =
optimizer=20
uses the selectivity of a predicate to estimate the cost of a particular =
access=20
method and to determine the optimal join order and join method.</P><A=20
name=3D27856></A>
<P class=3DBP>The statistics are stored in the data dictionary and can =
be exported=20
from one database and imported into another. For example, you might want =
to=20
transfer your statistics to a test system to simulate your production=20
environment.</P><A name=3D29293></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D29296></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D29297></A>
      <P class=3DNB>The statistics mentioned in this section are CBO =
statistics,=20
      not instance performance statistics visible through =
<CODE>V$</CODE>=20
      views.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A name=3D30297></A>
<P class=3DBP>You should gather statistics periodically for objects =
where the=20
statistics become stale over time because of changing data volumes or =
changes in=20
column values. New statistics should be gathered after a schema object's =
data or=20
structure are modified in ways that make the previous statistics =
inaccurate. For=20
example, after loading a significant number of rows into a table, =
collect new=20
statistics on the number of rows. After updating data in a table, you do =
not=20
need to collect new statistics on the number of rows, but you might need =
new=20
statistics on the average row length.</P><A name=3D25341></A>
<P class=3DBP>Use the <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> package to generate=20
statistics.</P><A name=3D27890></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D27894></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
      Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D27895></A>
      <P class=3DNB><EM class=3DItalic><A class=3D"xlinkSRC ARPLS059"=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/appdev.920/a966=
12/d_stats.htm#ARPLS059">Oracle9i=20
      Supplied PL/SQL Packages and Types=20
Reference</A></EM></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A =
name=3D25296></A>
<P class=3DBP>Statistics generated include the following:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25274></A>Table statistics=20
  <UL class=3DLD2>
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D25275></A>Number of rows=20
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D25276></A>Number of blocks=20
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D25278></A>Average row length =
</LI></UL>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25279></A>Column statistics=20
  <UL class=3DLD2>
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D25280></A>Number of distinct =
values (NDV)=20
    in column=20
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D30430></A>Number of nulls in =
column=20
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D30431></A>Data distribution =
(histogram)=20
    </LI></UL>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D30437></A>Index statistics=20
  <UL class=3DLD2>
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D25284></A>Number of leaf =
blocks=20
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D25285></A>Levels=20
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D25019></A>Clustering factor =
</LI></UL>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D28287></A>System statistics=20
  <UL class=3DLD2>
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D28735></A>I/O performance =
and utilization=20
    <LI class=3DLD2 type=3DCIRCLE><A name=3D28736></A>CPU performance =
and utilization=20
    </LI></UL></LI></UL><A name=3D26713></A><!--TOC=3Dh1-"26713"-->
<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Generating=20
Statistics</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A name=3D25599></A>
<P class=3DBP>Because the cost-based approach relies on statistics, you =
should=20
generate statistics for all tables and clusters and all indexes accessed =
by your=20
SQL statements before using the cost-based approach. If the size and =
data=20
distribution of the tables change frequently, then regenerate these =
statistics=20
regularly to ensure the statistics accurately represent the data in the=20
tables.</P><A name=3D25600></A>
<P class=3DBP>Oracle generates statistics using the following =
techniques:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25601></A>Estimation based on =
random data=20
  sampling=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25602></A>Exact computation=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25603></A>User-defined =
statistics collection=20
  methods </LI></UL><A name=3D29308></A>
<P class=3DBP>To perform an exact computation, Oracle requires enough =
space to=20
perform a scan and sort of the table. If there is not enough space in =
memory,=20
then temporary space might be required. For estimations, Oracle requires =
enough=20
space to perform a scan and sort of only the rows in the requested =
sample of the=20
table. For indexes, computation does not take up as much time or =
space.</P><A=20
name=3D26693></A>
<P class=3DBP>Some statistics are computed exactly, such as the number =
of data=20
blocks currently containing data in a table or the depth of an index =
from its=20
root block to its leaf blocks.</P><A name=3D31603></A>
<P class=3DBP>Oracle Corporation recommends setting the=20
<CODE>ESTIMATE_PERCENT</CODE> parameter of the <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> =
gathering=20
procedures to <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE>.<CODE>AUTO_SAMPLE_SIZE</CODE> to =
maximize=20
performance gains while achieving necessary statistical accuracy.=20
<CODE>AUTO_SAMPLE_SIZE</CODE> lets Oracle determine the best sample size =
for=20
good statistics. For example, to collect table and column statistics for =
all=20
tables in the <CODE>OE</CODE> schema with auto-sampling:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D29843></A>EXECUTE =
DBMS_STATS.GATHER_SCHEMA_STATS('OE',DBMS_STATS.AUTO_SAMPLE_SIZE);
<A name=3D29867></A>
</PRE><A name=3D27917></A>
<P class=3DBP>To estimate statistics, Oracle selects a random sample of =
data. You=20
can specify the sampling percentage and whether sampling should be based =
on rows=20
or blocks. Oracle Corporation recommends using=20
<CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE>.<CODE>AUTO_SAMPLE_SIZE</CODE> for the sampling=20
percentage. When in doubt, choose row sampling.</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D26649></A><STRONG =
class=3DGlossaryTerm>Row=20
  sampling</STRONG> reads rows without regard to their physical =
placement on=20
  disk. This provides the most random data for estimates, but it can =
result in=20
  reading more data than necessary. For example, in the worst case a row =
sample=20
  might select one row from each block, requiring a full scan of the =
table or=20
  index.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D26652></A><STRONG =
class=3DGlossaryTerm>Block=20
  sampling</STRONG> reads a random sample of blocks and uses all of the =
rows in=20
  those blocks for estimates. This reduces the amount of I/O activity =
for a=20
  given sample size, but it can reduce the randomness of the sample if =
rows are=20
  not randomly distributed on disk. Block sampling is not available for =
index=20
  statistics. </LI></UL><A name=3D25606></A>
<P class=3DBP>When you generate statistics for a table, column, or =
index, if the=20
data dictionary already contains statistics for the object, then Oracle =
updates=20
the existing statistics. Oracle also invalidates any currently parsed =
SQL=20
statements that access the object.</P><A name=3D25607></A>
<P class=3DBP>The next time such a statement executes, the optimizer =
automatically=20
chooses a new execution plan based on the new statistics. Distributed =
statements=20
issued on remote databases that access the analyzed objects use the new=20
statistics the next time Oracle parses them.</P><A name=3D26301></A>
<P class=3DBP>When you associate a statistics type with a column or =
domain index,=20
Oracle calls the statistics collection method in the statistics type, if =
you=20
analyze the column or domain index.</P><A =
name=3D26451></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"26451"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Getting=20
Statistics for Partitioned Schema Objects</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A=20
name=3D26452></A>
<P class=3DBP>Partitioned schema objects can contain multiple sets of =
statistics.=20
They can have statistics that refer to any of the following:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D32367></A>The entire schema =
object as a whole=20
  (global statistics)=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D32371></A>An individual =
partition=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D32372></A>An individual =
subpartition of a=20
  composite partitioned object </LI></UL><A name=3D33840></A>
<P class=3DBP>Unless the query predicate narrows the query to a single =
partition,=20
the optimizer uses the global statistics. Because most queries are not =
likely to=20
be this restrictive, it is most important to have accurate global =
statistics.=20
Intuitively, it can seem that generating global statistics from =
partition-level=20
statistics is straightforward; however, this is true only for some of =
the=20
statistics. For example, it is very difficult to figure out the number =
of=20
distinct values for a column from the number of distinct values found in =
each=20
partition, because of the possible overlap in values. Therefore, =
actually=20
gathering global statistics with the <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> package is =
highly=20
recommended, rather than calculating them with the <CODE>ANALYZE</CODE>=20
statement.</P><A name=3D36113></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D36103></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D36104></A>
      <P class=3DNB>Oracle Corporation strongly recommends that you use =
the=20
      <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> package rather than <CODE>ANALYZE</CODE> =
to=20
      collect optimizer statistics. That package lets you collect =
statistics in=20
      parallel, collect global statistics for partitioned objects, and =
fine tune=20
      your statistics collection in other ways. Further, the cost-based=20
      optimizer will eventually use only statistics that have been =
collected by=20
      <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE>. See <EM class=3DItalic><A =
class=3D"olinkSRC ARPLS"=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/appdev.920/a966=
12/toc.htm">Oracle9i=20
      Supplied PL/SQL Packages and Types Reference</A></EM> for more =
information=20
      on this package. <A name=3D36110></A></P>
      <P class=3DNB>However, you must use the <CODE>ANALYZE</CODE> =
statement=20
      rather than <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> for statistics collection not =
related=20
      to the cost-based optimizer, such as:</P>
      <UL class=3DNL>
        <LI class=3DNL type=3Ddisc><A name=3D36111></A>To use the=20
        <CODE>VALIDATE</CODE> or <CODE>LIST CHAINED ROWS</CODE> clauses=20
        <LI class=3DNL type=3Ddisc><A name=3D36112></A>To collect =
information on=20
        freelist blocks </LI></UL>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A =
name=3D33861></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"33861"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Using the=20
DBMS_STATS Package</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D33864></A>
<P class=3DBP>The PL/SQL package <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> lets you =
generate and=20
manage statistics for cost-based optimization. You can use this package =
to=20
gather, modify, view, export, import, and delete statistics. You can =
also use=20
this package to identify or name statistics gathered.</P><A =
name=3D26479></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> package can gather statistics =
on=20
indexes, tables, columns, and partitions, as well as statistics on all =
schema=20
objects in a schema or database. It does not gather cluster =
statistics--you can=20
use <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> to gather statistics on the individual =
tables=20
instead of the whole cluster.</P><A name=3D26480></A>
<P class=3DBP>The statistics-gathering operations can run either =
serially or in=20
parallel. Index statistics are not gathered in parallel.</P><A =
name=3D26488></A>
<P class=3DBP>For partitioned tables and indexes, =
<CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> can=20
gather separate statistics for each partition, as well as global =
statistics for=20
the entire table or index. Similarly, for composite partitioning,=20
<CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> can gather separate statistics for =
subpartitions,=20
partitions, and the entire table or index. Depending on the SQL =
statement being=20
optimized, the optimizer can choose to use either the partition (or=20
subpartition) statistics or the global statistics.</P><A =
name=3D25828></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> gathers only statistics needed for =

cost-based optimization; it does not gather other statistics. For =
example, the=20
table statistics gathered by <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> include the number =
of rows,=20
number of blocks currently containing data, and average row length, but =
not the=20
number of chained rows, average free space, or number of unused data=20
blocks.</P><A name=3D26517></A>
