      <P class=3DTB>1</P></TD></TR>
  <TR class=3DFormal vAlign=3Dtop align=3Dleft>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36576></A>
      <P class=3DTB><CODE>Leaf blocks</CODE></P></TD>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36535></A>
      <P class=3DTB>25</P></TD></TR>
  <TR class=3DFormal vAlign=3Dtop align=3Dleft>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36529></A>
      <P class=3DTB><CODE>Leaf blocks/key</CODE></P></TD>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36531></A>
      <P class=3DTB>1</P></TD></TR>
  <TR class=3DFormal vAlign=3Dtop align=3Dleft>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36555></A>
      <P class=3DTB><CODE>Data blocks/key</CODE></P></TD>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36527></A>
      <P class=3DTB>1</P></TD></TR>
  <TR class=3DFormal vAlign=3Dtop align=3Dleft>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36521></A>
      <P class=3DTB><CODE>Distinct keys</CODE></P></TD>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36523></A>
      <P class=3DTB>100</P></TD></TR>
  <TR class=3DFormal vAlign=3Dtop align=3Dleft>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36537></A>
      <P class=3DTB><CODE>Clustering factor</CODE></P></TD>
    <TD class=3DFormal><A name=3D36519></A>
      <P class=3DTB>800 (8 * number of =
blocks)</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><A=20
name=3D31058></A><!--TOC=3Dh1-"31058"-->
<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Using=20
Statistics</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A name=3D25382></A>
<P class=3DBP>This section provides guidelines on how to use and view =
statistics.=20
This includes:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D33639></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#26228">Managing=20
  Statistics</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D26220></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#26226">Verifying=20
  Table Statistics</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25375></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#25115">Verifying=20
  Index Statistics</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25376></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32682">Verifying=20
  Column Statistics</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25377></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#27027">Using=20
  Histograms</A> </LI></UL><A name=3D26228></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"26228"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Managing=20
Statistics</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D26229></A>
<P class=3DBP>This section describes statistics tables and lists the =
views that=20
display information about statistics stored in the data =
dictionary.</P><A=20
name=3D26231></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"26231"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Using=20
Statistics Tables</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D26232></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> package lets you store =
statistics in a=20
statistics table. You can transfer the statistics for a column, table, =
index, or=20
schema into a statistics table and subsequently restore those statistics =
to the=20
data dictionary. The optimizer does not use statistics that are stored =
in a=20
statistics table.</P><A name=3D26233></A>
<P class=3DBP>Statistics tables enable you to experiment with different =
sets of=20
statistics. For example, you can back up a set of statistics before you =
delete=20
them, modify them, or generate new statistics. You can then compare the=20
performance of SQL statements optimized with different sets of =
statistics, and=20
if the statistics stored in a table give the best performance, you can =
restore=20
them to the data dictionary.</P><A name=3D26234></A>
<P class=3DBP>A statistics table can keep multiple distinct sets of =
statistics, or=20
you can create multiple statistics tables to store distinct sets of =
statistics=20
separately.</P><A name=3D26235></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"26235"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Viewing=20
Statistics</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D28114></A>
<P class=3DBP>Use the <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> package to view the =
statistics=20
stored in the data dictionary or in a statistics table. <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32528">Example&nbsp;3-2</A>=20
queries a statistics table.</P><A name=3D32528></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
3-2 Viewing=20
Statistics in a Statistics Table</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D30196></A>DECLARE
<A name=3D30197></A>  num_rows NUMBER;
<A name=3D30198></A>  num_blocks NUMBER;
<A name=3D30199></A>  avg_row_len NUMBER;
<A name=3D31065></A>
<A name=3D31066></A>BEGIN
<A name=3D30201></A>  -- retrieve the values of table statistics on =
OE.ORDERS
<A name=3D30202></A>  -- statistics table name: OE.SAVESTATS    =
statistics ID: TEST1
<A name=3D30203></A>
<A name=3D30226></A>  DBMS_STATS.GET_TABLE_STATS('OE','ORDERS',null,
<A name=3D30204></A>    'SAVESTATS','TEST1',
<A name=3D30205></A>    num_rows,num_blocks,avg_row_len);
<A name=3D30206></A>
<A name=3D30207></A>  -- print the values
<A =
name=3D30209></A>DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('num_rows=3D'||num_rows||',num_bloc=
ks=3D'||num_blocks||
<A name=3D30211></A>                     =
',avg_row_len=3D'||avg_row_len);
<A name=3D30194></A>END;
<A name=3D32536></A>
</PRE><A name=3D28128></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D28166></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D28167></A>
      <P class=3DNB>Statistics held in a statistics table are held in a =
form that=20
      is only understood by using <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> package.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A name=3D26240></A>
<P class=3DBP>To view statistics in the data dictionary, query the =
appropriate=20
data dictionary view (<CODE>USER_</CODE>, <CODE>ALL_</CODE>, or=20
<CODE>DBA_</CODE>). The following list shows the <CODE>DBA_</CODE> =
views:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D29816></A><CODE>DBA_TABLES</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D26242></A><CODE>DBA_TAB_COL_STATISTICS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D26243></A><CODE>DBA_INDEXES</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D26244></A><CODE>DBA_CLUSTERS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D26245></A><CODE>DBA_TAB_PARTITIONS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D26246></A><CODE>DBA_TAB_SUBPARTITIONS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D26247></A><CODE>DBA_IND_PARTITIONS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D26248></A><CODE>DBA_IND_SUBPARTITIONS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D26249></A><CODE>DBA_PART_COL_STATISTICS</CODE>=20

  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A=20
  name=3D26250></A><CODE>DBA_SUBPART_COL_STATISTICS</CODE>=20
  <P><A name=3D26262></A></P>
  <DIV align=3Dcenter>
  <TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
  cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
  summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
    <TBODY>
    <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
      <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D26253></A><FONT=20
        face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
        Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D26254></A>
        <P class=3DNB><EM class=3DItalic><A class=3D"olinkSRC REFRN"=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
36/toc.htm">Oracle9i=20
        Database Reference</A></EM> for information on the statistics in =
these=20
        views</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV></LI></UL><A =
name=3D26226></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"26226"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Verifying=20
Table Statistics</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D30016></A>
<P class=3DBP>To verify that the table statistics are available, query =
the data=20
dictionary view <CODE>DBA_TABLES</CODE>, using a statement like the one =
in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32604">Example&nbsp;3-3</A>:</P><A=20
name=3D32604></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
3-3 Verifying=20
Table Statistics</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D30019></A>SQL&gt; SELECT TABLE_NAME, NUM_ROWS, BLOCKS, =
AVG_ROW_LEN,
<A name=3D30020></A>       TO_CHAR(LAST_ANALYZED, 'MM/DD/YYYY =
HH24:MI:SS')
<A name=3D30021></A>     FROM DBA_TABLES
<A name=3D30022></A>     WHERE TABLE_NAME IN =
('SO_LINES_ALL','SO_HEADERS_ALL','SO_LAST_ALL');
<A name=3D30023></A>
</PRE><A name=3D30024></A>
<P class=3DBP>This returns the following typical data:</P><PRE =
class=3DCEW><A name=3D30026></A>TABLE_NAME                 NUM_ROWS    =
BLOCKS  AVG_ROW_LEN        LAST_ANALYZED
<A name=3D30028></A>------------------------   --------   -------  =
-----------  -------------------
<A name=3D30030></A>SO_HEADERS_ALL              1632264    207014        =
  449  07/29/1999 00:59:51
<A name=3D30032></A>SO_LINES_ALL               10493845   1922196        =
  663  07/29/1999 01:16:09
<A name=3D30034></A>SO_LAST_ALL ...
<A name=3D32627></A>
</PRE><A name=3D30333></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D30086></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D30087></A>
      <P class=3DNB>Because the table <CODE>SO_LAST_ALL</CODE> has no =
statistics,=20
      it returns blanks for all columns.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A =
name=3D25115></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"25115"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Verifying=20
Index Statistics</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D25116></A>
<P class=3DBP>To verify that index statistics are available and decide =
which are=20
the best indexes to use in an application, query the data dictionary =
view=20
<CODE>DBA_INDEXES</CODE>, using a statement like the one in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32637">Example&nbsp;3-4</A>:</P><A=20
name=3D32637></A>
<H4 class=3DETW><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
3-4=20
Verifying Index Statistics</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D30150></A>SQL&gt; SELECT INDEX_NAME "NAME", NUM_ROWS, =
DISTINCT_KEYS "DISTINCT",
<A name=3D30151></A>  1  LEAF_BLOCKS, CLUSTERING_FACTOR "CF", BLEVEL =
"LEVEL",
<A name=3D30152></A>  2  AVG_LEAF_BLOCKS_PER_KEY "ALFBPKEY"
<A name=3D30153></A>  3  FROM DBA_INDEXES
<A name=3D30154></A>  4  WHERE OWNER =3D 'SH'
<A name=3D30155></A>  5* ORDER BY INDEX_NAME;
<A name=3D35720></A>
</PRE><A name=3D35719></A>
<P class=3DBP>Typical output is:</P><PRE class=3DCEW><A =
name=3D35394></A>NAME                       NUM_ROWS DISTINCT =
LEAF_BLOCKS      CF   LEVEL   ALFBPKEY
<A name=3D35395></A>-------------------------- -------- -------- =
----------- ------- ------- ----------
<A name=3D30159></A>CUSTOMERS_PK                  50000    50000         =
454    4405       2          1
<A name=3D30160></A>PRODUCTS_PK                   10000    10000         =
 90    1552       1          1
<A name=3D30161></A>PRODUCTS_PROD_CAT_IX          10000        4         =
 99    4422       1         24
<A name=3D30162></A>PRODUCTS_PROD_SUBCAT_IX       10000       37         =
170    6148       2          4
<A name=3D30163></A>SALES_PROD_BIX                 6287      909        =
1480    6287       1          1
<A name=3D30164></A>SALES_PROMO_BIX                4727      459         =
570    4727       1          1
<A name=3D30165></A>
<A name=3D30167></A>6 rows selected.
</PRE><A name=3D25135></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"25135"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Optimizer=20
Index Determination Criteria</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D25136></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer uses the following criteria when determining =
which=20
index to use:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25137></A>Number of rows in the =
index=20
  (cardinality).=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25138></A>Number of distinct =
keys. These=20
  define the selectivity of the index.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25139></A>Level or height of the =
index. This=20
  indicates how deeply the data probe must search in order to find the =
data.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D25140></A>Number of leaf blocks =
in the index.=20
  This is the number of I/Os needed to find the desired rows of data.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D27815></A>Clustering factor =
(<CODE>CF</CODE>).=20
  This is the collocation amount of the index block relative to data =
blocks. The=20
  higher the CF, the less likely the optimizer is to select this index.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D29771></A>Average leaf blocks =
for each key=20
  (<CODE>ALFBKEY</CODE>). Average number of leaf blocks in which each =
distinct=20
  value in the index appears, rounded to the nearest integer. For =
indexes that=20
  enforce <CODE>UNIQUE</CODE> and <CODE>PRIMARY</CODE> <CODE>KEY</CODE>=20
  constraints, this value is always one. </LI></UL><A =
name=3D25143></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"25143"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Determining=20
if You Have Chosen the Right Index</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D27827></A>
<P class=3DBP>Use the following notes to assist you in deciding whether =
you have=20
chosen an appropriate index for a table, data, and query:</P><A =
name=3D25144></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"25144"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>DISTINCT</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A name=3D25912></A>
<P class=3DBP>Consider index <CODE>ap_invoices_n3</CODE>, having two =
distinct=20
keys. The resulting selectivity based on index =
<CODE>ap_invoices_n3</CODE> is=20
poor, and the optimizer is not likely to use this index. Using this =
index=20
fetches 50% of the data in the table. In this case, a full table scan is =
cheaper=20
than using index <CODE>ap_invoices_n3</CODE>.</P><A =
name=3D25145></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"25145"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Index Cost=20
Tie</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A name=3D26840></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer uses alphabetic determination. If the =
optimizer=20
determines that the selectivity, cost, and cardinality of two finalist =
indexes=20
is the same, then it looks at the names of the indexes and chooses the =
name that=20
begins with the lower alphabetic letter or number.</P><A =
name=3D32682></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"32682"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Verifying=20
Column Statistics</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D32683></A>
<P class=3DBP>To verify that column statistics are available, query the =
data=20
dictionary view <CODE>DBA_TAB_COL_STATISTICS</CODE>, using a statement =
like the=20
one in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32652">Example&nbsp;3-5</A>:</P><A=20
name=3D32652></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
3-5 Verifying=20
Column Statistics</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D25149></A>SQL&gt; SELECT COLUMN_NAME, NUM_DISTINCT, NUM_NULLS, =
NUM_BUCKETS, DENSITY
<A name=3D25150></A>FROM DBA_TAB_COL_STATISTICS
<A name=3D25151></A>WHERE TABLE_NAME =3D"PA_EXPENDITURE_ITEMS_ALL"
<A name=3D25152></A>ORDER BY COLUMN_NAME;
</PRE><A name=3D25153></A><A name=3D25154></A>
<P class=3DBP>This returns the following data:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D28911></A>COLUMN_NAME                    NUM_DISTINCT  NUM_NULLS =
NUM_BUCKETS    DENSITY=20
<A name=3D27373></A>------------------------------ ------------ =
---------- ----------- ----------=20
<A name=3D27374></A>BURDEN_COST                            4300      =
71957           1 .000232558=20
<A name=3D27375></A>BURDEN_COST_RATE                        675    =
7376401           1 .001481481=20
<A name=3D27376></A>CONVERTED_FLAG                            1   =
16793903           1          1=20
<A name=3D27377></A>COST_BURDEN_DISTRIBUTED_FLAG              2      =
15796           1         .5=20
<A name=3D27378></A>COST_DISTRIBUTED_FLAG                     2          =
0           1         .5=20
<A name=3D27379></A>COST_IND_COMPILED_SET_ID                 87    =
6153143           1 .011494253=20
<A name=3D27380></A>EXPENDITURE_ID                      1171831          =
0           1 8.5337E-07=20
<A name=3D27381></A>TASK_ID                                8648          =
0           1 .000115634=20
<A name=3D25166></A>TRANSFERRED_FROM_EXP_ITEM_ID        1233787   =
15568891           1 8.1051E-07=20
<A name=3D25167></A>
</PRE><A name=3D27391></A>
<P class=3DBP>Verifying column statistics are important for the =
following=20
conditions:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D32593></A>Join conditions=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D32594></A>When the =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause=20
  includes a column(s) with a bind variable; for example: <CODE>column x =
=3D=20
  :variable_y</CODE> </LI></UL><A name=3D32758></A>
<P class=3DBP>In these cases, you can use the stored column statistics =
to get a=20
representative cardinality estimation for the given expression.</P><A=20
name=3D32762></A>
<P class=3DBP>The following subsections examine the data returned by the =
query in=20
<A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32652">Example&nbsp;3-5</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D32763></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"32763"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>NUM_DISTINCT Column =
Statistic</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A=20
name=3D32764></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>NUM_DISTINCT</CODE> indicates the number of distinct =
values=20
for a column.</P><A name=3D25175></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"25175"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Low</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A name=3D32717></A>
<P class=3DBP>In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32652">Example&nbsp;3-5</A>,=20
the number of distinct values for the column <CODE>CONVERTED_FLAG</CODE> =
is 1.=20
In this case, this column has only one value. If there is a bind =
variable on=20
column <CODE>CONVERTED_FLAG</CODE> in the <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause (for =

example, <CODE>CONVERTED_FLAG =3D:variable_y</CODE>), then this leads to =
poor=20
selectivity, and <CODE>CONVERTED_FLAG</CODE> is a poor candidate to be =
used as=20
the index.</P><A name=3D32718></A>
<P class=3DBP>Column <CODE>COST_BURDEN_DISTRIBUTED_FLAG</CODE>:=20
<CODE>NUM_DISTINCT</CODE> =3D 2. Likewise, this value is low.=20
<CODE>COST_BURDEN_DISTRIBUTED_FLAG</CODE> is not a good candidate for an =
index=20
unless there is much skew or there are a lot of nulls. If there is data =
skew of,=20
say, 90%, then 90% of the data has one particular value and 10% of the =
data has=20
another value. If the query only needs to access the 10%, then a =
histogram is=20
needed on that column in order for the optimizer to recognize the skew =
and use=20
an index on this column.</P><A name=3D25178></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"25178"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>High</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A name=3D27254></A>
<P class=3DBP>In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32652">Example&nbsp;3-5</A>,=20
<CODE>NUM_DISTINCT</CODE> is more than 1 million for column=20
<CODE>EXPEDITURE_ID</CODE>. If there is a bind variable on column=20
<CODE>EXPENDITURE_ID</CODE>, then this leads to high selectivity =
(implying high=20
density of data on this column). In other words, =
<CODE>EXPENDITURE_ID</CODE> is=20
a good candidate to be used as the index.</P><A =
name=3D32739></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"32739"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>NUM_NULL=20
Column Statistic</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D32740></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>NUM_NULLS</CODE> indicates the number of null values =
for that=20
column.</P><A name=3D32741></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"32741"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Low</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A name=3D25951></A>
<P class=3DBP>If a single column index has few nulls, such as the=20
<CODE>COST_DISTRIBUTED_FLAG</CODE> column in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32652">Example&nbsp;3-5</A>,=20
and if this column is used as the index, then the resulting data set is=20
large.</P><A name=3D25182></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"25182"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>High</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A name=3D25952></A>
<P class=3DBP>If there are many nulls on a particular column, such as =
the=20
<CODE>CONVERTED_FLAG</CODE> column in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32652">Example&nbsp;3-5</A>,=20
and if this column is used as the index, then the resulting data set is =
small.=20
This means that <CODE>COST_DISTRIBUTED_FLAG</CODE> is a more appropriate =
column=20
to index.</P><A name=3D25184></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"25184"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>DENSITY=20
Column Statistic</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D25185></A>
<P class=3DBP>This indicates the density of the values of that column. =
This is=20
calculated as 1 divided by <CODE>NUM_DISTINCT</CODE>.</P><A =
name=3D25186></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"25186"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Column=20
Statistics and Join Methods</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D25187></A>
<P class=3DBP>Column statistics are useful to help determine the most =
efficient=20
join method, which, in turn, is also based on the number of rows =
returned.</P><A=20
name=3D27027></A><!--TOC=3Dh1-"27027"-->
<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Using=20
Histograms</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A name=3D27032></A>
<P class=3DBP>The cost-based optimizer can use data value histograms to =
get=20
accurate estimates of the distribution of column data. A <STRONG=20
class=3DGlossaryTerm>histogram</STRONG> partitions the values in the =
column into=20
bands, so that all column values in a band fall within the same range.=20
Histograms provide improved selectivity estimates in the presence of =
data skew,=20
resulting in optimal execution plans with nonuniform data =
distributions.</P><A=20
name=3D27040></A>
<P class=3DBP>One of the fundamental tasks of the cost-based optimizer =
is=20
determining the selectivity of predicates that appear in queries. =
Selectivity=20
estimates are used to decide when to use an index and the order in which =
to join=20
tables. Some attribute domains (a table's columns) are not uniformly=20
distributed.</P><A name=3D27041></A>
<P class=3DBP>The cost-based optimizer uses height-based histograms on =
specified=20
attributes to describe the distributions of nonuniform domains. In a=20
height-based histogram, the column values are divided into bands so that =
each=20
band contains approximately the same number of values. The useful =
information=20
that the histogram provides, then, is where in the range of values the =
endpoints=20
fall.</P><A name=3D27052></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D27044></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
      Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D27050></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#27102">"Types=20
      of Histograms"</A><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#27102"></A></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D27053></A>
<P class=3DBP>Consider a column C with values between 1 and 100 and a =
histogram=20
with 10 buckets. If the data in C is uniformly distributed, then the =
histogram=20
looks similar to <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#35723">Figure&nbsp;3-1</A>,=20
where the numbers are the endpoint values.</P><A name=3D35723></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
3-1 Histogram=20
with Uniform Distribution</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D27057><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf208.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf208.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf208.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf208.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D27058></A>
<P class=3DBP>The number of rows in each bucket is one tenth the total =
number of=20
rows in the table. Four-tenths of the rows have values between 60 and =
100 in=20
this example of uniform distribution.</P><A name=3D27059></A>
<P class=3DBP>If the data is not uniformly distributed, then the =
histogram might=20
look similar to <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#35726">Figure&nbsp;3-2</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D35726></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
3-2 Histogram=20
with Non-Uniform Distribution</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D27063><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf209.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf209.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf209.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf209.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D27064></A>
<P class=3DBP>In this case, most of the rows have the value 5 for the =
column; only=20
1/10 of the rows have values between 60 and 100.</P><A =
name=3D27065></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"27065"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>When to Use=20
Histograms</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D29512></A>
<P class=3DBP>Histograms can affect performance and should be used only =
when they=20
substantially improve query plans. Histogram statistics data is =
persistent, so=20
the space required to save the data depends on the sample size. In =
general,=20
create histograms on columns that are used frequently in =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clauses of queries and have a highly skewed data distribution. For =
uniformly=20
distributed data, the cost-based optimizer can make fairly accurate =
guesses=20
about the cost of executing a particular statement without the use of=20
histograms.</P><A name=3D27068></A>
<P class=3DBP>Histograms, like all other optimizer statistics, are =
static. They=20
are useful only when they reflect the current data distribution of a =
given=20
column. (The data in the column can change as long as the <EM=20
class=3DItalic>distribution</EM> remains constant.) If the data =
distribution of a=20
column changes frequently, you must recompute its histogram =
frequently.</P><A=20
name=3D27069></A>
<P class=3DBP>Histograms are not useful for columns with the following=20
characteristics:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D27070></A>All predicates on the =
column use=20
  bind variables.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D36222></A>The column data is =
uniformly=20
  distributed.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D36225></A>The column is unique =
and is used=20
  only with equality predicates. </LI></UL><A =
name=3D35016></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"35016"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Creating=20
Histograms</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D27980></A>
<P class=3DBP>You generate histograms by using the =
<CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE>=20
package. You can generate histograms for columns of a table or =
partition. For=20
example, to create a 10-bucket histogram on the <CODE>SAL</CODE> column =
of the=20
<CODE>emp</CODE> table, issue the following statement:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D28252></A>EXECUTE DBMS_STATS.GATHER_TABLE_STATS=20
<A name=3D27521></A>('scott','emp', METHOD_OPT =3D&gt; 'FOR COLUMNS SIZE =
10 sal');
<A name=3D27522></A>
</PRE><A name=3D27523></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>SIZE</CODE> keyword declares the maximum number =
of buckets=20
for the histogram. You would create a histogram on the <CODE>SAL</CODE> =
column=20
if there were an unusually high number of employees with the same salary =
and few=20
employees with other salaries. You can also collect histograms for a =
single=20
partition of a table.</P><A name=3D27992></A>
<P class=3DBP>Oracle Corporation recommends using the =
<CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE>=20
package to have the database automatically decide which columns need =
histograms.=20
This is done by specifying <CODE>SIZE</CODE> <CODE>AUTO</CODE>.</P><A=20
name=3D27538></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D27531></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH><STRONG=20
      class=3DBold>See Also</STRONG>:</STRONG></FONT> <A =
name=3D27532></A>
      <P class=3DNB><EM class=3DItalic><A class=3D"xlinkSRC ARPLS059"=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/appdev.920/a966=
12/d_stats.htm#ARPLS059">Oracle9i=20
      Supplied PL/SQL Packages and Types Reference</A></EM> for more =
information=20
      on the <CODE>DBMS_STATS</CODE> =
package</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D27539></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"27539"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Choosing=20
the Number of Buckets for a Histogram</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A=20
name=3D27540></A>
<P class=3DBP>If the number of frequently occurring distinct values in a =
column is=20
relatively small, then set the number of buckets to be greater than that =
number.=20
The default number of buckets for a histogram is 75. This value provides =
an=20
appropriate level of detail for most data distributions. However, =
because the=20
number of buckets and the data distribution both affect a histogram's=20
usefulness, you might need to experiment with different numbers of =
buckets to=20
obtain optimal results.</P><A name=3D27102></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"27102"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Types of=20
Histograms</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D27103></A>
<P class=3DBP>There are two types of histograms:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D27107></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#27113">Understanding=20
  Height-Based Histograms</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D27111></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#27119">Understanding=20
  Value-Based Histograms</A> </LI></UL><A =
name=3D27113></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"27113"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Understanding Height-Based =
Histograms</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A=20
name=3D29968></A>
<P class=3DBP>Height-based histograms place approximately the same =
number of=20
values into each range, so that the endpoints of the range are =
determined by how=20
many values are in that range. Only the last (largest) values in each =
bucket=20
appear as bucket (end point) values.</P><A name=3D27115></A>
<P class=3DBP>Consider that a table's query results in the following =
four sample=20
values: 4, 18, 30, and 35.</P><A name=3D27116></A>
<P class=3DBP>For a height-based histogram, each of these values =
occupies a=20
portion of one bucket, in proportion to their size. The resulting =
selectivity is=20
computed with the following formula:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D27117></A>S =3D Height(35) / Height(4 + 18 + 30 + 35)
</PRE><A name=3D27119></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"27119"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Understanding Value-Based =
Histograms</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A=20
name=3D29915></A>
<P class=3DBP>Value-based histograms are created when the number of =
distinct=20
values is less than or equal to the number of histogram buckets =
specified. In=20
value-based histograms, all the values in the column have corresponding =
buckets,=20
and the bucket number reflects the repetition count of each value. These =
can=20
also be known as frequency histograms.</P><A name=3D29530></A>
<P class=3DBP>Consider the same four sample values in the previous =
example. In a=20
value-based histogram, a bucket is used to represent each of the four =
distinct=20
values. In other words, one bucket represents 4, one bucket represents =
18,=20
another represents 30, and another represents 35. The resulting =
selectivity is=20
computed with the following formula:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D27121></A>S =3D [#rows(35)/(#rows(4) + #rows(18) + #rows(30) + =
#rows(35))] / #buckets
<A name=3D27122></A>
</PRE><A name=3D27123></A>
<P class=3DBP>If there are many different values anticipated for a =
particular=20
column of table, it is preferable to use the value-based histogram =
rather than=20
the height-based histogram. This is because if there is much data skew =
in the=20
height, then the skew can offset the selectivity calculation and give a=20
nonrepresentative selectivity value.</P><A =
name=3D27124></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"27124"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Using=20
Histograms</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D27125></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32833">Example&nbsp;3-6</A>=20
illustrates the use of a histogram in order to improve the execution =
plan and=20
demonstrate the skewed behavior of the <CODE>s6</CODE> indexed =
column.</P><A=20
name=3D32833></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
3-6 Using a=20
Histogram to Improve an Execution Plan</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D29142></A>UPDATE so_lines l=20
<A name=3D27127></A>SET open_flag=3Dnull,=20
<A name=3D27128></A>     s6=3D10,=20
<A name=3D27129></A>     s6_date=3Dsysdate,=20
<A name=3D27130></A>WHERE l.line_type_code in =
('REGULAR','DETAIL','RETURN') AND=20
<A name=3D27131></A>      l.open_flag =3D 'Y' AND =
NVL(l.shipped_quantity, 0)=3D0 OR=20
<A name=3D27132></A>      NVL(l.shipped_quantity, 0) !=3D 0 AND=20
<A name=3D27133></A>      l.shipped_quantity +NVL(l.cancelled_quantity, =
0)=3D l.ordered_quantity)) AND=20
<A name=3D27134></A>      l.s6=3D18
<A name=3D27135></A>
</PRE><A name=3D27136></A>
<P class=3DBP>This query shows the skewed distribution of data values =
for=20
<CODE>s6</CODE>. In this case, there are two distinct non-null values: =
10 and=20
18. The majority of the rows consists of <CODE>s6</CODE> =3D 10 =
(1,589,464), while=20
a small number of rows consist of <CODE>s6</CODE> =3D 18 =
(13,091).</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D28914></A>S6:      COUNT(*)
<A =
name=3D27138></A>=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D
<A name=3D27139></A>10        1,589,464
<A name=3D27140></A>18           13,091
<A name=3D27141></A>NULL         21,889=20
<A name=3D27143></A>
</PRE><A name=3D27485></A>
<P class=3DBP>The selectivity of column <CODE>s6</CODE>, where =
<CODE>s6</CODE>=20
<CODE>=3D</CODE> <CODE>18</CODE>:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D29222></A>S =3D 13,091 / (13,091 + 1,589,464) =3D 0.008
<A name=3D32846></A>
</PRE><A name=3D27146></A>
<P class=3DBP>If No Histogram is Used: The selectivity of column =
<CODE>s6</CODE>=20
is assumed to be 50%, uniformly distributed across 10 and 18. This is =
not=20
selective; therefore, <CODE>s6</CODE> is not an ideal choice for use as =
an=20
index.</P><A name=3D27147></A>
<P class=3DBP>If a Histogram is Used: The data distribution information =
is stored=20
in the dictionary. This allows the optimizer to use this information and =
compute=20
the correct selectivity based on the data distribution. In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/stats.htm#32833">Example&nbsp;3-6</A>,=20
the selectivity, based on the histogram data, is 0.008. This is a =
relatively=20
high, or good, selectivity, which leads the optimizer to use an index on =
column=20
<CODE>s6</CODE> in the execution plan.</P><A =
name=3D27451></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"27451"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Viewing=20
Histograms</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D32890></A>
<P class=3DBP>To view histogram information, query the appropriate data =
dictionary=20
view (<CODE>USER_</CODE>, <CODE>ALL_</CODE>, or <CODE>DBA_</CODE>). The=20
following list shows the <CODE>DBA_</CODE> views:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D29145></A><CODE>DBA_HISTOGRAMS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D27455></A><CODE>DBA_PART_HISTOGRAMS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D27456></A><CODE>DBA_SUBPART_HISTOGRAMS</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D27457></A><CODE>DBA_TAB_COL_STATISTICS</CODE>=20
  </LI></UL><A name=3D27458></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"27458"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Number of=20
Rows</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A name=3D27459></A>
<P class=3DBP>View the <CODE>DBA_HISTOGRAMS</CODE> dictionary table for =
the number=20
of buckets (in other words, the number of rows) for each column:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D27460></A><CODE>ENDPOINT_NUMBER</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D27461></A><CODE>ENDPOINT_VALUE</CODE>=20
  <P><A name=3D27471></A></P>
  <DIV align=3Dcenter>
  <TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
  cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
